Yes 👍 you are thinking in the right direction — let’s make it clear step by step.

🔁 Correct Troubleshooting Flow
✅ Step 1: Ping

👉 Checks basic network reachability

ping 125.17.6.13

✔ If reply comes → network path is reachable
❌ If not → routing/firewall issue

✅ Step 2: Telnet

👉 Checks port connectivity

telnet 125.17.6.13 443

✔ If connected → port is open
❌ If failed → firewall / port block / whitelist issue

✅ Step 3: Curl

👉 Checks application/API level connectivity

curl https://api.whatsapp.com

✔ If response comes → API is reachable
❌ If timeout/403/SSL issue → application-level issue

🎯 Now your main question:
❓ “Can we use curl after ping & telnet to identify whitelist issue?”
✔ YES — but with understanding:

👉 Curl helps confirm real API-level behavior, not just network reachability.

⚠️ Important clarity
❌ Ping/Telnet only tells:
Network is reachable
Port is open

BUT

❌ They DO NOT confirm:
API access allowed
IP is whitelisted
Application is responding correctly
✔ Curl tells:
Is API accepting request?
Is IP allowed (whitelist check)?
Is SSL/API working?
Is timeout happening at application layer?
🧠 In your case (very important)

If:

Ping ✔
Telnet ✔
Curl ❌ (timeout / forbidden)

👉 Then most likely:
✔ IP not whitelisted
✔ Firewall blocking API
✔ Application-level restriction

💡 Simple conclusion

👉 Yes, curl is the final and most important test after ping & telnet

Because:

Ping = basic network
Telnet = port check
Curl = real API/whitelist validation
🧠 One-line memory trick

👉 Ping → reachability
Telnet → port
Curl → real service (whitelist/API)

What NAT does

👉 NAT = Network Address Translation

It:

Changes Private IP → Public IP
Keeps track of sessions
Maps response back to correct internal system
📌 Example
Request:
172.24.11.172 → 125.17.6.13
After NAT:
203.x.x.x (Airtel Public IP) → 125.17.6.13
⚠️ Why this matters in your issue

If:

NAT IP not whitelisted ❌
Firewall blocks it ❌

👉 Then:

SYN goes out ✔
Response fails ❌
TCP handshake breaks ❌
Timeout happens
